## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design online dynamic order-statistics with batch inserts, focusing on tangible metrics (inversions) and practical data structures, not abstract theory.

## Key Concepts

- Inversions, Fenwick tree, coordinate compression, online batch processing, duplicates handling, memory trade-offs.

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.bit=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  update(i,delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.bit[i]+=delta; }
  query(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.bit[i]; return s; }
}
// assume valueToRank and rankToValue are defined, U is number of ranks
let bit = new Fenwick(U);
let totalInv = 0;
let seen = 0;
for (const x of batch){
  const r = valueToRank[x];
  totalInv += seen - bit.query(r);
  bit.update(r, 1);
  seen++;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle deletions or updates to previous elements?
- How would you adapt if the value domain is unbounded or values arrive online without prior compression?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity with top-k heavy hitters in streaming data, bounded memory, and batch updates. Tests ability to reason about error guarantees and practical trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming top-k with bounded memory
- Space-Saving / Frequent Items algorithms
- Per-element vs batch cost
- Error guarantees: ε ≤ total_count/k
- Trade-offs vs exact O(M) memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class SpaceSaving {
  constructor(k){ this.k=k; this.map=new Map(); this.counterHeap=[]; }
  update(x){ if(this.map.has(x)){ this.map.get(x).cnt++; return; }
    if(this.map.size < this.k){ this.map.set(x,{cnt:1}); return; }
    // replace min counter item
    let minKey = null, minCnt = Infinity;
    for(const [key,v] of this.map){ if(v.cnt < minCnt){ minCnt=v.cnt; minKey=key; } }
    this.map.delete(minKey);
    this.map.set(x,{cnt:minCnt+1});
  }
  topK(){ // shallow: return current keys with counts
    return Array.from(this.map.entries()).sort((a,b)=>b[1].cnt-a[1].cnt).map(([k,v])=>({key:k,count:v.cnt}));
  }
}
```

